HaLow: ignore regulatory hints that do not match the country modparam

The morse wiphy is REGULATORY_WIPHY_SELF_MANAGED.  Its country is driven
solely by the 'country' module parameter (set by ucentral/hostapd_s1g
when bringing up HaLow).  When the 2.4/5G radio changes country (e.g.
user pushes FR then GB), the kernel broadcasts a global user regulatory
hint to ALL wiphys.  morse_reg_notifier() would then overwrite
mors->country (to GB), even though the HaLow modparam is EU.  On the
next morse_mac_ops_add_interface() the consistency check finds
mors->country(GB) != country(EU) and returns -EPERM, killing wlan0.

Fix: in morse_reg_notifier(), if the incoming hint does not match the
loaded country modparam, log and ignore it.  This makes the modparam the
single source of truth and prevents 2/5G regulatory changes from
interfering with HaLow operation.

This does NOT affect the normal country-change flow (JSON pushes a new
HaLow country → module is reloaded with a new modparam value → register-
time regdomain set in morse_mac_register, handled by patch 023).

Signed-off-by: EdgeCore <dev@edge-core.com>

--- a/mac.c
+++ b/mac.c
@@ -7313,6 +7313,20 @@ static void morse_reg_notifier(struct wiphy *wiphy, struct regulatory_request *re
 	char *req_cc;
 	int i;
 
+	/*
+	 * EdgeCore: this wiphy is REGULATORY_WIPHY_SELF_MANAGED.  The HaLow
+	 * country is authoritative from the 'country' modparam (ucentral
+	 * normalizes European codes to EU before loading the module).
+	 * Ignore any regulatory hint that does not match our modparam;
+	 * this prevents 2.4/5G country changes (e.g. GB, FR) from mutating
+	 * mors->country and causing add_interface -EPERM.
+	 */
+	if (!country_codes_are_equal(request->alpha2, country)) {
+		MORSE_INFO(mors, "Ignoring regulatory hint %c%c (modparam country=%c%c)\n",
+			   request->alpha2[0], request->alpha2[1], country[0], country[1]);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (test_bit(MORSE_STATE_FLAG_REGDOM_SET_BY_OTP, &mors->state_flags)) {
 		MORSE_WARN(mors, "Regulatory change ignored, hardware restricted to %c%c\n",
 			   mors->country[0], mors->country[1]);
